{
  "slug": "talking-signs",
  "title": "Talking Signs",
  "year": "1979",
  "subtitle": "The invisible city: infrared beams broadcasting spoken directions that only you can hear, turning the built environment into an ambient interface for blind navigation.",
  "why_scout_cares": "This is ambient HCI decades before the term existed. Instead of putting a computer on the user, Talking Signs embedded intelligence into the environment — a network of infrared transmitters that broadcast spoken labels on modulated light beams. A blind person carrying a pocket-sized receiver could 'read' the city by pointing it around: a doorway would whisper 'Market Street BART entrance,' a bus would announce its route number, an ATM would explain how to use it. The interaction model is remarkably elegant: the signal gets clearer and louder as you point toward it, providing directional feedback that guides you physically toward your destination. It used infrastructure as interface — and it worked well enough to become US federal building code (ADAAG 703.7) and spawn a 40-year research lineage that continues today as the free overTHERE smartphone app. The 1991 technical manual (the full Fall 1991 issue of the Smith-Kettlewell Technical File) is a beautiful primary source with complete schematics, deployment philosophy, and installation guides. This is the story of making the world speak — and the economics that kept it from speaking everywhere.",
  "search_terms": [
    "Talking Signs remote infrared signage 1979",
    "William Loughborough Talking Lights",
    "Smith-Kettlewell Talking Signs infrared",
    "remote infrared audible signage ADA",
    "SKTF Fall 1991 Talking Signs",
